Summary:


- 🔥The Angular templating system gives us a powerful syntax to express the dynamic part of our
    HTML. It allows to express data and property binding, event binding and templating concerns, in a
    clear way, each with their own symbols:
        • {{}} for interpolation
        • [] for property binding
        • () for event binding
        • # for variable declaration
        • @if/@for/@switch for the control flow
    
    It takes some time to be fluent in this syntax, but you will soon be up to speed, and then it’s easy
    to read and write. Let’s go through a complete example before moving on.


- example: ✨I want to write a Ponies component, displaying a list of ponies. ✨Each pony should be
    represented by a Pony component, ✨but we haven’t seen yet how to pass parameters to a component. So,
    for now, we are going to display a simple list. ✨The list should be displayed only if it’s not empty,
    and ✨I’d like to have some color for the even lines of my list. ✨Finally, I want to be able to
    refresh the list with a button click.

    We'll have a folder structure:

        src/app/
            └── ponies/
                ├── models/
                │    └── pony.model.ts
                │
                ├── ponies/
                │    ├── ponies.ts
                │    ├── ponies.html
                │
                └── pony/
                    ├── pony.ts
                    ├── pony.html
    
    1️⃣First let's create all the required components and interface:
        🔥 To create a ponies component inside a ponies folder: 
                ng g c ponies/
                
        🔥 To create a pony component inside a ponies folder:
                ng g c ponies/pony

        🔥 To create an interface PonyModel inside a models folder inside a ponies folder: 
                src/app/ponies/models/pony-model.ts
        

    2️⃣Let's update the Ponies components next:

            import { Component, signal } from '@angular/core';
            import { PonyModel } from '../models/pony-model';

            @Component({
                selector: 'ns-ponies',
                standalone: true,
                template: `
                    <button (click) = "refreshPonies()">Refresh</button>
                    <ul>
                        @for (pony of ponies(); track pony.id) {
                        <li [style.color]="$even ? 'green' : 'black'">{{pony.name}}</li>
                        }
                    </ul>`,
                imports: [],
            })
            export class Ponies {
                protected readonly ponies = signal<PonyModel[]>([ 
                    { id: 1, name: 'Rainbow Dash' },
                    { id: 2, name: 'Pinkie Pie' }
                ]);
                
                protected refreshPonies() {
                    this.ponies.set([
                    { id: 3, name: 'Twilight Sparkle' },
                    { id: 4, name: 'Applejack' }
                    ]);
                }
            }

        
        🔥(learned from section 3): we import PonyModel interface to use it in the signal.

        🔥(learned from Chapter 8, section 3): Notice that when creating signal ponies we did not use
            the syntax:
                signal<PonyModel>([..])
            Instead we used:
                signal<PonyModel[]>([..])
            🔥It's because the ESLint perfers PonyModelModel[] mainly because it's cleaner and more
            readable and also to avoid nesting confusion. 
            PonyModel[][] is much easier to read than Array<Array<PonyModel>>
        
        🔥(learned from section 6): Next, we added the button in the template which triggers the 
            refreshPonies() method. refreshPonies() method updates the ponies signal with a new list of
            ponies. (see section Chapter 8, section 3 for more details about signals)

        🔥Lastly, a touch of color to finish, with the use of [style.color] and the $even variable in
            the template.


    3️⃣Our Ponies component is ready Now we need to use ponies component in our app component

            import { Component, signal } from '@angular/core';
            import { Ponies } from './ponies/ponies/ponies';

            @Component({
                selector: 'ns-root',
                imports: [Ponies],
                templateUrl: './app.html',
                styleUrl: './app.css'
            })
            export class App {
                protected readonly title = signal('ponyracer');
            }

        🔥we import the Ponies component into our app component.

        🔥imports: [Ponies], gives a warning ⚠️'All imports are unused(-998113)' because in Angular 
            15+ (and especially Angular 17+ with @for), this rule applies:
                If you use imports: []
                THEN the component MUST be standalone
            Otherwise Angular ignores imports entirely and treats your component like a classic module
            component.

        🔥✅To fix the warning, we need to make our Ponies component standalone by adding standalone: true
            in the @Component.
    

    4️⃣ Finally we can use <ns-ponies></ns-ponies> inside the app template or inside the app.html to
        display the list of ponies. I've decided to use it inside the app.html:

            <div class="app">
                <h1>Hello, {{ title() }}</h1>
                <br>
                <ns-ponies></ns-ponies>
            </div>
    
    5️⃣ ng serve the application and see the result in the browser. You should see the list of ponies with
        text color black and green, and when you click on the refresh button, the list should update
        with the new ponies.(see ponyracer app inside Chapter 7)


- ⚠️Note that we did not use Pony component because we haven’t seen yet how to pass parameters to a
    component. So, for now, we only displayed a simple list.